[mcpeek]

.NET assembly browser panel plugin

mcpeek shows a managed assembly as the tree its metadata already forms:
references, namespaces, types, members, embedded resources.  Nothing is
unpacked and no external tool is needed to walk it.

An assembly inside an archive, a container or a remote panel is opened the
same way: it is copied out first, because an assembly is read by mapping it
and a stream has no file behind it.  The copy lives as long as the panel.

Opening an assembly:

  Enter on a .dll, .exe or .winmd   through the magic.ini rule that
                                    matches what libmagic calls a
                                    Mono/.Net assembly
  Ctrl-PgDn on the same file        without a rule
  F9 -> Left/Right -> .NET assembly browser
                                    opens the current directory as a set
                                    of assemblies

The panel:

  Enter, F3   on a member shows it as C#, with the cursor on the member
  Shift-F3    shows the IL the C# was made from
  F4          opens the same text in the editor
  F5          on a member copies that text out; on a type writes that
              type as one .cs; on a namespace or Properties/ writes
              what is listed there, one file at a time; on an assembly
              of the set copies the file itself.  A text that cannot be
              had is a failed copy, not a file with a note in it
  Alt-F5      writes the whole assembly as a compilable project, with
              its PDB, into a directory it asks for
  Alt-F7      on a type, a method or a field lists where it is used
  Ctrl-R      re-reads the assembly after it has been rebuilt and
              returns to the type the panel stood in, found by name

Levels:

  a directory      every managed assembly in it
  an assembly      with a decompiler, the directory the sources would
                   form: one directory per namespace holding a .cs per
                   type, the project file, Properties/, and IL/; without
                   one, the metadata tree itself, and no IL/
  a namespace      under the root, the .cs of its types; under IL/, the
                   types themselves
  IL               namespaces, types and members, with tokens and sizes
  a type           fields, properties, events, methods and nested types
  References       what the assembly needs, with the file each name
                   resolves to; an unresolved one is marked "missing"
                   and cannot be entered

A .cs is decompiled when it is opened, not when it is listed, so a listing
costs nothing however large the assembly.  The project file and
Properties/AssemblyInfo.cs are the only two that a whole-project export
produces and nothing else does; that export is written once into the cache
and kept for as long as the assembly keeps its mtime.

Columns:

  Kind    what the entry is: asm, ns, type, method, field, prop, event,
          ref, res, use
  Token   the metadata token, as it appears in the IL
  Size    the size of a method body, in bytes of IL

C# and export

C# comes from an external decompiler; without one the plugin shows IL and
nothing else is lost.  It is looked for in mcpeek.ini, then on PATH, then
in ~/.dotnet/tools, which is where a dotnet global tool lands and where
PATH usually does not reach.  The command can be set in the settings
dialog: F9 -> Command -> Manage plugins, then Enter on mcpeek.

A run of the decompiler costs about a second, so a whole type is
decompiled at once and kept until the assembly changes on disk.

An export (Alt-F5) writes the portable PDB beside the sources, so the
result can be opened in a debugger and not only read.  The line numbers
of the written files are made to agree with that PDB, which means a
breakpoint set on the line you see stops on the statement you meant.

Where a type or member is used

Alt-F7 lists every place the selected entry is used, across the assemblies
this panel has opened: the ones in the directory it started from and the
ones reached through a reference.  Enter on a result opens the type that
declares the using method.  The key can be changed under [keys] in
mcpeek.ini.

The search reads metadata and IL only, so it costs a fraction of a second
and needs no decompiler.  A caller names what it uses through a reference
assembly, which usually does not declare the type but exports it and says
which assembly really has it, so those forwardings are followed; a
reference to an assembly not installed here is taken at face value rather
than dropped.

Assembly resolution

A reference is looked for beside the assembly first, then under
runtimes/ next to it, then in the shared frameworks and reference packs
of every dotnet installation found, then in the NuGet cache.  What is not
installed on this machine stays unresolved, which is an ordinary outcome
and not an error.
